traceability.evidence:
1) Story and traceability - Story file exists: docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md - Dedicated test file explicitly tied to this story: tests/rules/auto-fix-behavior-008.test.ts - Header: "* Tests for: docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
 * @story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md
 * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING ...
 * @req REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT ..."
2) Auto-fix for missing @story annotations (REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING, REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE, REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE) - Rule implementation: src/rules/require-story-annotation.ts - Meta enables ESLint auto-fix: "meta: { ..., hasSuggestions: true, fixable: 'code', messages: { missingStory: 'Function '{{name}}' must have an explicit @story annotation...' }, ... }" - Traceability to this story and requirement: "@story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md" "@req REQ-AUTOFIX-MISSING - This rule supports auto-fixing missing @story annotations per Story 008.0 auto-fix behavior." - Fixer helpers: src/rules/helpers/require-story-core.ts - Built-in template via ANNOTATION constant (from require-story-helpers.ts): "const STORY_PATH = 'docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md';
const ANNOTATION = `/** @story ${STORY_PATH} */`;" - Function fix: "export function createAddStoryFix(target: any) { ... return fixer.insertTextBeforeRange([start, start], `${ANNOTATION}
`); }" - Method fix: "export function createMethodFix(node: any) { ... return fixer.insertTextBeforeRange([start, start], `${ANNOTATION}
 `); }" - Reporting helpers wire fixers into ESLint reports: src/rules/helpers/require-story-helpers.ts - reportMissing(...): "context.report({ node: nameNode, messageId: 'missingStory', data: { name, functionName: name }, fix: createAddStoryFix(resolvedTarget), suggest: [{ desc: `Add JSDoc @story annotation for function '${name}', e.g., ${ANNOTATION}`, fix: createAddStoryFix(resolvedTarget) }] });" - reportMethod(...) similarly uses createMethodFix - hasStoryAnnotation(...) uses multiple heuristics (JSDoc, preceding comments, leading comments, nearby text, parent chain) and aborts reporting/fixing if an annotation already exists, preventing duplicate or unsafe edits - Tests verifying auto-fix behavior: tests/rules/auto-fix-behavior-008.test.ts - Uses RuleTester with rule requireStoryRule - Valid cases assert already-annotated functions/methods are unchanged - Invalid cases assert exact before/after code for multiple patterns: - Function declaration: code: "function autoFixMe() {}", output: "/** @story docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md */
function autoFixMe() {}" - Function expression: code: "const fnExpr = function() {};", output: "/** @story docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md */
const fnExpr = function() {};" - Class method: code: "class C {
 method() {}
}", output: "class C {
 /** @story docs/stories/003.0-DEV-FUNCTION-ANNOTATIONS.story.md */
 method() {}
}" - TS declare function and TS method signature with TypeScript parser configured, each with expected output showing inserted placeholder @story - Additional coverage: tests/rules/require-story-annotation.test.ts exercises same behaviors (missing annotations → added @story line) under various options (exportPriority, scope) with explicit expected output, confirming integration of fixers across configurations
3) Auto-fix for simple @story format issues / path suffix normalization (REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT, REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE, REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE) - Rule implementation: src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts - Rule-level traceability: "@story docs/stories/008.0-DEV-AUTO-FIX.story.md" "@req REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT - Provide safe, minimal automatic fixes for common format issues" "@req REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE - Auto-fix behavior must be conservative and non-destructive" - Meta flags fix support, with explicit scope description: "meta: { ..., fixable: 'code', ... }" and doc comment: "This rule's fixable support is limited to safe @story path suffix normalization per Story 008.0. Fixes are limited strictly to adjusting the suffix portion ... preserving all other comment text and whitespace." - Suffix normalization helper: src/rules/helpers/valid-annotation-utils.ts - getFixedStoryPath: "if (original.includes('..')) return null;" (avoid traversal) "if (/\.story\.md$/.test(original)) return null;" (already correct) "if (/\.story$/.test(original)) return `${original}.md`;" (add .md) "if (/\.md$/.test(original)) return original.replace(/\.md$/, '.story.md');" (normalize .md → .story.md) "return `${original}.story.md`;" (add .story.md when no extension) - Function is annotated with Story 008.0 and requirements REQ-AUTOFIX-FORMAT, REQ-AUTOFIX-SAFE, REQ-AUTOFIX-PRESERVE - Comment-level fix application: createStoryFix in src/rules/valid-annotation-format.ts - Computes replacement range limited to the @story value: - Finds "@story" index, then matches the following non-whitespace segment and uses comment.range plus offsets to compute [start, end] - Returns a function that calls fixer.replaceTextRange(fixRange, fixed) - If tag or value cannot be safely located, returns null → prevents unsafe fixes - reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix(...) only uses fix when createStoryFix succeeds; otherwise delegates to reportInvalidStoryFormat (no fix) - validateStoryAnnotation(...) decides whether to fix: - If no value: reports invalidStoryFormat ("missing") with no fix - If value has whitespace: calls reportInvalidStoryFormat (no fix) - Else: computes fixed = getFixedStoryPath(collapsed); only when fixed exists and matches the configured pattern does it call reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix (with fix), else reports without fix - Tests validating suffix auto-fix: - tests/rules/auto-fix-behavior-008.test.ts (Story 008 focus) - Valid case: already-correct story path unchanged - Invalid, fixable cases: - "adds .md extension for .story path": code: "// @story docs/stories/005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION.story", output: "// @story docs/stories/005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION.story.md" - "adds .story.md extension when missing entirely": code: "// @story docs/stories/005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION", output: "// @story docs/stories/005.0-DEV-ANNOTATION-VALIDATION.story.md" - tests/rules/valid-annotation-format.test.ts (broader rule tests) assert the same outputs for these patterns and additional config scenarios, confirming behavior is consistent and limited to suffix normalization
4) Safety and non-destructiveness (acceptance criteria: Quality Standards, Error Handling) - Missing-annotation fixes: - Only insert new `@story` comment lines using ANNOTATION template; do not edit existing comments or any code body - hasStoryAnnotation(...) checks multiple sources (JSDoc, getCommentsBefore, leadingComments, nearby lines, parent chain, fallback text) and skips reporting/fixing if any existing @story is found, avoiding duplicate or conflicting annotations - All fixer logic is wrapped in try/catch in reportMissing/reportMethod, so unexpected issues result in no fix rather than partial or corrupt edits - Path-format fixes: - getFixedStoryPath returns null for paths with ".." or already-correct suffix, so no fix is attempted in those cases - validateStoryAnnotation avoids fixes when values contain whitespace (more complex/multiline) or cannot be validated safely against the regex pattern - createStoryFix returns null when it cannot cleanly isolate the @story value; reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix then falls back to error-only reporting - These behaviors implement conservative, non-destructive fixes and graceful handling of non-fixable edge cases
5) Integration with ESLint and overall UX (Core Functionality, Integration, User Experience) - Rules are incorporated into the plugin's rule set and configs: src/index.ts - RULE_NAMES includes both "require-story-annotation" and "valid-annotation-format" and they are dynamically required into the rules object - TRACEABILITY_RULE_SEVERITIES marks them with severities used by recommended/strict configs; `valid-annotation-format` is configured as a warning to reduce noise while still fixable via `--fix` - The RuleTester-based tests exercise the rules through ESLint’s standard APIs with realistic parserOptions (including TypeScript parser where needed), demonstrating correct integration with the ESLint toolchain and with `--fix` - Auto-fix behavior is consistent across supported patterns (functions, function expressions, class methods, TS declarations and interfaces; simple story path strings), and valid cases confirm no changes when annotations/paths are already correct
6) Documentation of auto-fix capabilities (Documentation acceptance criterion) - user-docs/api-reference.md documents both rules’ auto-fix behavior in line with Story 008.0: - traceability/require-story-annotation section: "When run with `--fix`, the rule inserts a single-line placeholder JSDoc `@story` annotation above missing functions, methods, TypeScript declare functions, and interface method signatures using a built-in template aligned with Story 008.0... fixes are strictly limited to adding this placeholder annotation without altering the function body or changing any runtime behavior." - traceability/valid-annotation-format section: "When run with `--fix`, the rule limits changes to safe `@story` path suffix normalization only—for example, adding `.md` when the path ends with `.story`, or adding `.story.md` when the base path has no extension—using targeted replacements implemented in the `getFixedStoryPath` and `reportInvalidStoryFormatWithFix` helpers. It does not change directories, infer new story names, or modify any surrounding comment text or whitespace, in line with Story 008.0..." - This matches the story’s Documentation acceptance criterion and explicitly describes which issues are fixable and under what safety constraints
7) Out-of-scope requirements acknowledged as future work - The story itself marks REQ-AUTOFIX-TEMPLATE (configurable templates) and REQ-AUTOFIX-SELECTIVE (fine-grained toggling of fix types) as "Not yet implemented" and positions them as future enhancements, not part of this iteration’s Definition of Done - The current implementation matches that scope: it uses a fixed built-in template and standard ESLint rule-level enabling/disabling, with no additional per-fix-type toggles
